                    },
                    "message": "Yeah, that's easier on the eyes. Applied it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "tools/sdbusplus/templates/interface.common.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Maybe this would be a good place to put the actual 'char*' type so there's no ambiguity to anyone, but just a suggestion."
                },
                {
                    "file": "tools/sdbusplus/templates/interface.common.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "i tried it and apparently it's not a `char*` because this is what i get \ud83d\ude04\n\n```\ntest/gen/server/Test/common.hpp:50:44: error: ISO C++ forbids converting a string constant to \u2018char*\u2019 [-Werror=write-strings]\n   50 |         static constexpr char* someValue = \"SomeValue\";\n      |                                            ^~~~~~~~~~~\n```\n\nThanks for this idea, agree that the generated code should be verbose with the type for the benefit of the reader.\n\nI set it to be `std::string_view` for now because that's a type for which the example compiles. We can re-open this in case someone has a better idea for a type."
                },
                {
                    "file": "tools/sdbusplus/templates/interface.common.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Apparently if it's `const char*` it works out with the assignment. Just tested it out.\n\nSo you were in a way right all along, i was just missing the 'const' part."
                }
            ],
