            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "test/gen/test_property_names.cpp",
                    "line": 14,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Wow.  Us naming that test case \"server\" was a bad choice, wasn't it...\n\nI was thrown by \"common::server\"."
                },
                {
                    "file": "tools/sdbusplus/templates/interface.common.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I'd prefer if these are snake_case.  The async server and client code uses snake_case everywhere."
                },
                {
                    "file": "tools/sdbusplus/templates/interface.common.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I'd prefer an `auto` here.  Is there a reason to make it explicitly a string_view?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "tools/sdbusplus/templates/interface.common.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "We discussed it in https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/sdbusplus/+/84199/comment/5d8686b4_b7b94a45/\n\nand i assume it would be `const char*` if we used auto.\n\n```\n[alex@odroid tmp]$ g++ --std=c++23 main.cpp\nmain.cpp: In function \u2018int main()\u2019:\nmain.cpp:5:11: error: request for member \u2018k\u2019 in \u2018x\u2019, which is of non-class type \u2018const char*\u2019\n    5 |         x.k = 30;\n      |           ^\n[alex@odroid tmp]$ cat main.cpp \n\nint main(){\n\n\tauto x = \"hello\";\n\tx.k = 30;\n\n\treturn 0;\n}\n```\n\nnow we can go from one to the other without too much effort but `string_view` has a various methods which can be used on it and it's a more modern approach which also stores the size alongside the pointer.\n\nCan you explain why you prefer `auto`?\n\nDoes it not make the code harder to read, given we know the actual type?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "tools/sdbusplus/templates/interface.common.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "I went with it because that's what looked closest to the actual property name in a lot of cases. \n\nWill adjust in next revision."
                },
                {
                    "file": "tools/sdbusplus/templates/interface.common.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "1. I prefer \u201calmost-always-auto\u201d over \u201cexplicit types\u201d. This is a philosophical discussion about \u201cwhich is easier to read\u201d and you\u2019ll find plenty of pros and cons mused about elsewhere; I\u2019m not planning to enumerate those in detail.  \n\n2. \u201cauto\u201d implies \u201cthe compiler can choose what is optimal\u201d. Some compilers use const char* and some use const char[].  I\u2019d prefer to leave decisions to compilers rather than humans when humans don\u2019t really care. \n\n3. string_view, while \u201cmore modern\u201d is not required to be null terminated, which means it isn\u2019t as simple to use as most people assume.\n\n4. \u201cmore modern\u201d in this case doesn\u2019t even mean that the older one is deprecated. Even Rust has about 4 different ways to represent strings. \n\n5. Most of sdbusplus doesn\u2019t use (or accept) string_view anyhow, so it is ergonomically worse to have to use accessor functions everywhere because the functions you want to use the data with don\u2019t support sv."
                },
                {
                    "file": "tools/sdbusplus/templates/interface.common.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "tools/sdbusplus/templates/interface.common.hpp.mako",
                    "line": 45,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the detailed reasoning, changed as requested."
                }
            ],
